 "title": "Quanta of Space: A Manifesto",
 "description": "<p><i>To see a World in a Grain of Sand / And a Heaven in a Wild Flower, / Hold Infinity in the palm of your hand / And Eternity in an hour.</i> (William Blake, <i>Auguries of Innocence</i>)</p><p>Version 1.3: a new black cover in the style of the other books, the DICTO square with the title below in LED letters; the chapter on Aristotle's pyramids in the wording of the Collected Works; DICTO's ideas credited as proposed, specified or conjectured throughout. Version 1.2: the catalogue of all the atoms shows a picture for every shape that has one (model drawings, or the figure of its entry), pictures first and the few without at the end; the known and rediscovered shapes are listed at the end of the book; more space round titles. Version 1.1 corrected the name in About the author.</p><p>DICTO's manifesto and the overview of his geometric works: is space smooth, or built from exact pieces that fit with no gaps? From the atoms of Democritus and Plato's kit of solids to the quanta of modern physics, the book follows that question through 68 works, 66 papers in six chapters: Atoms, Bonds, Shells, Cycles, Stacking and Four dimensions, with the Kaleidohedra findings and the data set.</p><p>Its centre is the Rule of Halves: however equal balls are stacked in close-packed layers, the space between them is shared out into pieces of only finitely many kinds, every one borrowed whole or in half from the two pure stackings, a finite catalogue of quanta for every close packing, which closes into two tiles, HexOct. Chapter 1 ends with the catalogue of all the atoms: DICTO's 102 numbered shapes D1 to D102 and his 31 new clusters, structures and networks, 63 of them drawn from exact geometry; the known and rediscovered shapes the work builds with are listed at the end.</p><p>The works from #52 on are told in short; the books HexOct: The Laws of Stacking, The Golden Cycle and Alicia in Wonderland: The Red &amp; White Queens hold them in full. For my father, Alan Baker. DICTO is the artist name of (Alan) J. Baker. The cover is the DICTO square: the five Platonic solids of DICTO's name, D I C T O, in a five by five square, with the title below.</p><p>The papers, apps and data of this work are free to use; every one is open access, under the licence CC BY-SA 4.0. Computation, checking and drafting with Claude (Anthropic). More at <a href=\"https://dictospheres.com\">dictospheres.com</a>.</p>",
